  "problem": "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{\u2032}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{\u2033}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{\u2032}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2033}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{\u2032}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2033}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$",
